\paragraph{Shared multi-layer memory.} The agents share no conversation history. They share a memory that code assembles from the system's own record in seven layers, read by the Portfolio Manager every morning. L1 holds the day's typed analyst signals. L2 gives each holding its entry date, days held, entry thesis and its last seven Technical ratings. L3 is the narrative: the last seven evening outlooks, seven days of macro regime, and the high-conviction news state changes of the last two weeks. L4 is a 45-day calibration table: code matches buys to later exits first-in-first-out and reports win rate, mean return and holding time by entry size (below \$5k, \$5--10k, above \$10k), so that the agent learns whether its conviction pays. L5 lists the last five Risk Manager verdicts with their categories, L6 the agent's own last three decisions, so that reversals are visible, and L7 a projected book that flags sectors approaching their cap. Every number an agent can cite therefore comes from code, not from another agent's prose.

\paragraph{Schema-enforced reasoning.} Every agent's output type contains its reasoning procedure as a nested CoT object in which each step is a field declared \texttt{str = Field(min\_length=1)} (Listing~\ref{lst:schema}; Appendix~\ref{app:cot} lists every agent's steps). An agent cannot skip a step, enumerations are \texttt{Literal} types, and cross-field validators encode domain invariants: an actionable rating must carry an entry and a stop on the protective side, a \texttt{wrong} grade must name a root cause, and a proposal must cite a number. List-valued fields are validated item by item, so a malformed entry is dropped and logged instead of invalidating a whole report. The validated object is stored verbatim with its exact input, so the record is not a log \emph{about} decisions but the decisions themselves, in a form that code can grade, a reflector can read and a harness can replay.

