\paragraph{Security of LLM agents.}
Prompt injection was characterised as goal hijacking~\cite{perez2022ignore} and, in its indirect form, as an attack delivered through retrieved Web content~\cite{greshake2023injection}; a formal treatment followed~\cite{liu2024formalizing}, and benchmarks show that agents remain vulnerable~\cite{zhan2024injecagent,debenedetti2024agentdojo,zhang2025asb}. Recent defences are architectural: execution isolation~\cite{wu2025isolategpt}, capability and information-flow control~\cite{debenedetti2025camel,costa2025fides}, and design patterns that bound what an agent may do after reading untrusted input~\cite{beurerkellner2025patterns}. Our invariant core is of this architectural kind, and our setting adds a channel beyond the memory poisoning it studies~\cite{zhang2025asb}: the agent's own record fed back to a reflector that rewrites its instructions.

