Strict convexity supports the smooth, type-dependent quality adjustments underlying the baseline characterization of Proposition~\ref{prop:lambda_utility}, but it is not necessary for utility polarization itself. If quality were costless and unconstrained, the platform's pointwise virtual surplus $\mathcal{V}(\theta;q,\alpha)$ would be unbounded in $q$ for any type with a positive marginal virtual benefit; with an independently fixed finite technological ceiling, the platform would instead choose maximum feasible quality whenever this marginal benefit is positive. More generally, under linear costs ($c(q)=\mu q$), quality becomes a threshold decision: provide maximum feasible quality if the marginal virtual benefit exceeds $\mu$, and zero otherwise. Linear costs therefore replace the baseline's smooth, type-dependent quality adjustments with boundary choices. Advertising and information-rent effects can nevertheless still generate utility polarization, so strict convexity shapes the form of the baseline allocation rather than serving as a necessary condition for the polarization result.
