Returns and market values come from the CRSP daily and monthly stock files \citep{crspstockdatabase}, and the Fama--French factors and risk-free rate from the Kenneth R.\ French Data Library \citep{frenchlibrary}. The universe consists of the 1{,}000 largest ordinary common shares (share codes 10 and 11 on NYSE, AMEX and Nasdaq) by market value at the end of each June from 2010 to 2024, and each list is used from that June until the next. We hold every daily return of the 2{,}062 stocks that enter a list, from 2011 to 2024. Portfolios are formed at the close of the last trading day of each month, fortnight or week. A stock enters a period if it is on the current list and has traded in the last five days, which gives about 984 stocks per period and panels of 141{,}761 stock-months, 306{,}746 stock-fortnights and 614{,}525 stock-weeks. A stock that stops trading during a holding period earns zero for the rest of it. The return file matters. From 2017 on, an earlier extract held daily returns only for stocks on the current calendar year's June list, so from January to June a stock about to leave the list had no recent returns, and such stocks went on to underperform by 3\% to 5\% a month. A tree model learned to find them, and the resulting weekly net Sharpe ratio of 1.56 on price features alone came from this gap. The complete file removes this, and no stock in our panels lacks a recent return.

