\subsection{Geometric Selectivity}

The mass assigned to a given target depends on how strongly the other candidates agree with one another.
The following result makes this dependence explicit.

Fix a target candidate with unit direction $u$ and prior mass $g\in(0,1)$.
Write $v_i$ for at most $n\ge2$ remaining directions and $w_i$ for their conditional weights, so $w_i\ge0$ and $\sum_iw_i=1$.
Define their mean affinity to the target and their internal agreement by
\begin{equation}
 \chi=\sum_iw_i e^{u^\top v_i-1},\qquad
 \mathcal B=\sum_{i,j}w_iw_j e^{v_i^\top v_j-1}.
 \label{eq:gaf-affinities}
\end{equation}
We consider the regime where $\chi$ is at most $e^{-1}$, the kernel value for orthogonal directions.
This is a condition on the weighted mean affinity; individual candidates may have larger affinities to the target.

